Because our enemies are a more target-rich environment than we are.  
Governments can't hide, move, or look innocent and inoffensive.  They are 
large, fat, juicy, targets that are worth the powder to blow them up with. 
 We are not.  We never will be because the power of markets is diffuse but 
the power of governments is concentrated.  If they are delayered and 
downsized by whatever means--we win.  

I don't expect violence to play much of a role.  The governments' 
conversion into market entities is more likely.  This may be followed by 
their decline into bankruptcy as they find it hard to compete on the 
market.  

I know that you will consider it unlikely that a government can be forced 
to become a market actor but unless they can grab the new markets on the 
nets, those can grow exponentially outside of traditional regulations and 
swamp the remaining government functions by size and wealth.

Thought experiment -- Imagine that Gore succeeds and we get some sort of 
"tame" New Information Infrastructure.  A tame market grows on the nets.  
It doubles in size.  It doubles again.  It doubles again.  Ten years pass. 
 How big is it?  The international forex markets tripled in daily volume 
between 1986 and 1992.  At some point, no matter how "tame" it starts (and 
I doubt it will start very tame) it becomes a 400 pound gorilla and 
totally out of control because no man can tame it not even a government.

Example from the Economist special supplement "The World in 1994" on 
newsstands now:

"A call between Europe and the US costs a tiny $0.025 a minute.  Check 
your telephone bill to see the huge margins your phone company is lifting 
off of you.  Capitalism has ways of dealing with such abuses.  A new breed 
of telecoms carrier--the resellers--will pop up everywhere next 
year....It's legal, it's cheap and it will bring down traditional 
telephone charges across the world next year."

Note that most of those telephone monopolies being smashed are government 
companies.  It doesn't matter.  They're history together with Indian State 
Television and the French restrictions on US programming.  Those rules are 
*gone* (or going).  

