Date: Mon, 27 Dec 1993 12:13:32 -0500 (EST)
From: usr1593a@tso.uc.EDU (Bruce Zimov)
Subject: FOUNDATION: Technology/Politics

Extropianism appears to loosely integrate a political agenda with a
technological research programme.  Now, I know research programmes are
theory-laden, and presuppose scientific and sometimes economic and social
values, but I thought that the relationship between what values go with
what research programmes was NOT 1-1!!

In other words, why can't transhuman technology be value-neutral at this
time, i.e. politically speaking.  Why can't we just have a blind mad dash
to bring it about, and let the rest sort itself out when the future gets
here? That's the way it usually happens.  Or, why can't we see the other
political agendas emerge and compete memetically for what to do with the
technology.

I think the blind dash has at least one justification, that is that the
genetic dash does OK blind as a complex competitive phenomena, the memetic
dash should too. The only thing is that the memes sometimes don't get
free competition when open-mindedness is not encouraged.

I think that anything that delays the coming technology is bad, and 
anything that accelerates it is good. At this point, extropianism gets
a plus for raising the levels of awareness of transhumanity, and minuses
for thinking that any one political agenda is the "answer" to the
question of the proper stance toward the technology.

