  By your own decision statement listed above, a political viewpoint is
chosen. Any political system which delays technological advancement is
bad, any which accelerates it is good. Centralized management and control
of an economy is notoriously bad for technology advancement and deployment
while free-markets are good. 

  Capitalism naturally promotes diversity through free markets while
socialism stiffles it. Thus, under a free market you will see more
memetic expression than under a socialist one because a socialist
system has no competition nor natural selection process. So in the
US, you can buy 100 different brands of scented soap with something
for everyone's taste while in the former USSR you probably can't buy
any.

 The only way to have the "mad dash" as you so call it is to call for
laissez-faire. Any dash which is controlled/regulated by the government
is not mad at all.

 Most extropians I have talked to seem to feel a natural connection between
free-market economics, genetics, ecology, complexity theory, game theory,
space exploration, etc but they often can not put the whole connection into 
words. I think it all has to do genetic algorithms and evolution. That
natural selection, competition, and new offspring (whether memes, or
new people) can produce better answers than any centralized mechanism.

 Consider the awesome task of maximizing everyone's utility function by
allocating production among the whole nation. This is most likely
NP complex, probably computionally impossible even if you knew what
everyone's utility function was at any point in time. Consider the task of
allocating research capital among the whole of your economy. Do you really
think any group of bureaucrats or scientists is going to know what works?
No. The only way to know is to try, try, and try again, hundreds of
different ideas each competing and being selected out. The government
by its very nature is slow and conservative. Research projects are hardly
ever terminated even when it's clear that they are losers. Lobbyists prevent
losers from dying gracefully for decades. (would the free market ever
tolerate disasters like the SSC and Freedom which spent billions on
paper designs before any hardware was ever built?)

 The free market is the exact opposite being ruthlessly cold about terminating
many projects when they don't turn a profit. And turning a profit is about
the only real, objective, measurable value of success. "Greater good" is
immeasurable.

 So you see, free-market philosophy is not just good for technology, but
mathematically it's much more feasible than attempting a top-down
optimization. It also bears a strong resemblance to genetic algorithms
and evolutionary memes which are a big part of Extropian philosophy.

 No one on this list has ever made a rational case for an alternative
to markets. If you wish to make one, go ahead, but pleading to us
for consideration of other political ideas is not going to work. This
is a market, and you're going to have to "sell" us on the idea, otherwise
we don't care.

  Most of us favor capitalism because we think capitalism will provide
life extension technology faster than any other system will. US 
Biotech companies have already provided far more new drugs and vaccines
(for example, new aids vaccines) than the NIH ever will. Of course, the
government hates that, that's why there's a new AIDS bill in congress
that calls for the confiscation of private biotech research by the
government for possible AIDS cure usage. (under the guise of Emminent Domain
"just compensation" of course)

-Ray, future gun-carrying Nazi, capitalist-pig in training

p.s. Besides the utilitarian justification for our political agenda, there
is also a ethical one as well (e.g. property rights are natural, schelling
point, and violating them is unethical)


