I think people will want their kids to be above average in school, but not
intimidatingly so, so they will "fit in".  So I think we'll see the average
IQ go to 120.  Then, after that generation grows up, they'll want their
kids to be above average, so the average IQ wil go to 140...

If it turns out that a gene can predispose to homosexuality, people will
put lots of effort into making sure that gene doesn't get into their kids. 
And you don't have to be homophobic to want to do that, though that
certainly helps.  I don't think I would want my kids to part of a despised
minority, and less likely to produce grandchildren.

An interseting question is what will happen if an edited child costs
$20,000 up front to produce.  This would be enough that poor people would
not be able to afford it.  Then we would have a self-perpetuating
hereditary upper class that really was superior.

>What I'm looking at is what sort of self-transformation people
>seem to want for themselves, and what they support in terms of
>schools. This suggests that what most people (in the US) want is 
>"good" looks and atheletic ability. (Anyone have similar guesses
>about what traits might be popular in other countries or cultures?)

Masculinity.  

In India, amniocentesis followed by selective abortion of female embryos
seems to be quite popular among the upper classes.  I believe the
government has made it illegal, but I don't think that is having much
effect.  

In China, the offcial census says that 113 boys are being born for every
100 girls.  Apparently infanticide or neglect cuts down on the number of
surviving female infants.  The true ratio probably isn't that skewed, since
some of the census bias is due to people underreporting the birth of girls.
 But even if the true ratio is 105:100, this is going to have some social
effects in twenty years or so.  

The technology for sex selection is available here in the US, but nobody
seems to do it, because of a concensus in the medical community that it is
a bad idea.

There's a book on this topic called "Gendercide".  It came out about 5
years ago, so it's a little outdated, but it's worth finding in a library
if you're interested in this topic.

>Actually, if we're just talking about survival/ability to influence
>the future, maybe we should be talking about designing children
>to be good at influencing other people while being willing to
>take some losses on verbal/math intelligence if necessary, especially
>if there are still suckers out there who are willing to raise the
>technicians. 

Just to inject a note of grim reality into this discussion...

There is definite evidence for heritability of IQ, schizophrenia, and
possibly of intoversion/extroversion, but I'm not aware of any evidence of
genetic influence for subtle traits of temprament like "be good at
influencing other people".  It would be cool if that was possible, though.

We don't know any genes for any mental characteristics, except for gross
biochemical defects like startle disorders and Huntington's disease.  The
highly publicized discoveries of genes for schizophrenia and manic
depression could not be replicated and appear to be erroneous.  The "gene
for homosexuality" theory is based on a very small sample, and I'd like to
see it replicated before I believe it.


