On Mon, 27 Dec 1993, Nick Szabo wrote:

> Tim May writes: 
> > Markets for organ transplants can similary be set up off-shore or via
> > remailers and anonymous transaction services. 
> 
> The medical procedures themselves usually cannot be performed
> anonymously.  Quality medical care usually requires medical
> histories, often requires genetic typing (eg for donor matching), etc.
> Anon transaction services make it easy for the medical provider (or
> an employee with acess to their records) to sell information on
> their patients untraceably.
> 
> Offshore data havens are thus ideal for collecting information about
> who has had abortions, genetic therapy, and other controversial
> medical services.  This data can be made available to insurers, political 
> opponents of these medical procedures, prospective lovers, etc.
> Cf. abortion opponents who "executed" the abortion doctor for "murder" -- 
> an early example of inter-PPL justice.  If one's bosses, customers,
> or suppliers are strongly opposed to procedures one has undergone,
> this data can be used for blackmail.  In this mileau, very controversial 
> medical procedures may be effectively "outlawed" by terrorists (PPLs in 
> their own eyes) highly motivated by intolerance-producing memes.

Any good capitalist could see some problems with this argument. For instance,
I assume that the for those people stupid enough to use their real names and
identities to use these services, then they would have a vested interest in
keeping their privacy.

A group looking to buy a list of people who used such and such a service would
be paying some amount per name. The service would get in touch (somehow) with
the people on it's list notifying them that they are on a list, and can
avoid the release of information by paying more money. It is actually cheaper
for the person to do this, than for the single buyer to buy all the names,
and so the service makes more money and the list becomes 'shorter'...

Only thing to watch for is surveillance of communications to try to see where
all these messages are going. We're already seeing the use of remailers, PGP,
etc. now, so I hardly see this as insurmountable...

Of course a person could avoid all this by staying anonymous... let the buyer
beware (of off-shore agents)... Also, I doubt a monopoly would exist, so
competition in keeping customers coming back would be high. Breaches of trust
and lack of privacy could kill a business like this, especially with the
widespread growth of agencies which keep tabs on businesses like this...

It's doubtful a business wants to kill it's customers just to make a bit more
money by selling a list to people who may or may not ever even use your
business in the long run...

